movement.

263
00:15:17,760 --> 00:15:20,460
Turn the yoke left or right to move the ailerons on the wings

264
00:15:20,560 --> 00:15:25,460
up and down, making the jet bank to one side or the other.

265
00:15:25,560 --> 00:15:28,880
Pull the yoke back or push it forward, and the elevator on the

266
00:15:28,980 --> 00:15:34,420
jet's tail moves up or down, making the jet climb or descend.

267
00:15:34,520 --> 00:15:37,580
That covers the flight basics, but the water drop requires

268
00:15:37,680 --> 00:15:40,020
more support.

269
00:15:40,021 --> 00:15:42,560
That comes from the pilot of a scout aircraft near the fire

270
00:15:42,660 --> 00:15:47,120
zone, who offers advice on the best angle of attack.

271
00:15:47,220 --> 00:15:49,060
Then it's all up to the DC-10 pilot

272
00:15:49,160 --> 00:15:53,260
to make a run as low as 200 feet above the flames

273
00:15:53,360 --> 00:15:57,880
and drop the load of water with pinpoint accuracy.

274
00:15:57,980 --> 00:15:59,680
We have three separate tanks.

275
00:15:59,780 --> 00:16:02,380
So what we're going to call out to the captain

276
00:16:02,480 --> 00:16:09,120
is we have three tanks set at 50%, coverage level 5,

277
00:16:09,121 --> 00:16:12,220
And these will all be five, three green lights.

278
00:16:12,320 --> 00:16:15,360
Pre-drop checklist is complete.

279
00:16:15,460 --> 00:16:16,260
Stand by.

280
00:16:20,160 --> 00:16:23,560
Every move this crew makes has to be perfect.

281
00:16:23,660 --> 00:16:27,080
When they're over the target, the press of one button

282
00:16:27,180 --> 00:16:30,280
relays the drop sequence to the storage tanks.

283
00:16:30,380 --> 00:16:32,880
Coming up to our drop start point.

284
00:16:32,980 --> 00:16:33,780
Hit that button.

285
00:16:33,880 --> 00:16:35,020
That button's live now.

286
00:16:35,120 --> 00:16:35,820
Drop.

287
00:16:35,821 --> 00:16:36,880
Drop.

288
00:16:36,980 --> 00:16:38,280
The crew can program the tanks

289
00:16:38,281 --> 00:16:41,580
drop their 12,000 gallons of water in a drizzle covering

290
00:16:41,680 --> 00:16:48,520
a wide area, or a downpour confined to a specific zone.

291
00:16:48,620 --> 00:16:51,500
But shedding 50 tons of water in just eight seconds

292
00:16:51,600 --> 00:16:53,700
can send the plane swooping upward,

293
00:16:53,800 --> 00:16:56,840
like cresting up the hill of a roller coaster.

294
00:16:56,940 --> 00:17:00,200
And it's up to the pilot to instantly adjust.

295
00:17:00,300 --> 00:17:01,600
We're dropping.

296
00:17:01,700 --> 00:17:05,400
I'm using my left thumb to push the trim,

297
00:17:05,401 --> 00:17:10,800
to turn the nose down, and I'm pushing the yoke forward

298
00:17:10,900 --> 00:17:15,140
because loss of weight transfers the center of gravity,

299
00:17:15,240 --> 00:17:19,640
and the plane wants to leap up into the air.

300
00:17:19,740 --> 00:17:21,840
Months can pass between wildfires.

301
00:17:21,940 --> 00:17:24,560
But during peak fire season, the Victorville tanker crew

302
00:17:24,660 --> 00:17:28,260
might fly as many as eight or nine missions in a single day.

303
00:17:28,360 --> 00:17:29,760
Weekly training runs like this one

304
00:17:29,860 --> 00:17:32,660
keep them sharp until that crucial time arrives.

305
00:17:32,760 --> 00:17:34,400
DC-10 and the firefighting role

306
00:17:34,401 --> 00:17:37,640
has been the most satisfying flying I've ever done.

307
00:17:37,740 --> 00:17:42,140
Not only do we get to enjoy the flying of flying a large aircraft

308
00:17:42,240 --> 00:17:46,240
close to the ground and flying it closer to its limits,

309
00:17:46,340 --> 00:17:49,840
but we also get to do something that is helping.

310
00:17:55,760 --> 00:17:59,860
For an airborne driver's seat with a little less heat,

311
00:17:59,960 --> 00:18:01,900
take to the skies in the serene cockpit

312
00:18:01,901 --> 00:18:05,700
of a helium-filled airship.

313
00:18:05,800 --> 00:18:08,700
Behold the Eureka, one of a new generation

314
00:18:08,800 --> 00:18:10,500
of German-made Zeppelins.

315
00:18:10,600 --> 00:18:15,140
With a semi-rigid frame, it's 14 feet longer than a 747.

316
00:18:15,240 --> 00:18:18,620
And each of its 14 seats aboard, including the pilots,

317
00:18:18,720 --> 00:18:19,420
is first class.

318
00:18:23,360 --> 00:18:24,660
It's not a blimp.

319
00:18:24,760 --> 00:18:25,860
It is an airship.

320
00:18:25,960 --> 00:18:30,660
And the main difference is that a blimp is a envelope

321
00:18:30,661 --> 00:18:34,460
with no rigid structure inside, with the gondola suspended

322
00:18:34,560 --> 00:18:38,000
from the envelope. In our case, we have a rigid airframe

323
00:18:38,100 --> 00:18:41,200
made of aluminum and carbon fiber.

324
00:18:41,300 --> 00:18:43,200
Unlike a blimp, the engines are attached

325
00:18:43,300 --> 00:18:45,700
to its semi-rigid frame far above the cabin,

326
00:18:45,800 --> 00:18:48,120
not to the cabin itself.

327
00:18:53,920 --> 00:18:57,060
Based near San Francisco, it flies sightseers

328
00:18:57,160 --> 00:19:00,660
over Silicon Valley.

329
00:19:00,661 --> 00:19:03,160
Look at this incredible view.

330
00:19:03,260 --> 00:19:05,260
You don't find that in an airplane, for example,

331
00:19:05,360 --> 00:19:08,760
or in a helicopter, and it's not so loud.

332
00:19:08,860 --> 00:19:11,300
I mean, we can even have a nice conversation.

333
00:19:14,900 --> 00:19:17,620
Any licensed pilot who takes a two-day training course

334
00:19:17,720 --> 00:19:20,620
can test out this driver's seat for a 30-minute flight.

335
00:19:22,720 --> 00:19:23,720
Roger.

336
00:19:26,360 --> 00:19:28,660
We've had everything from 747 captains

337
00:19:28,661 --> 00:19:34,580
747 captains with 30,000 hours, all the way down to private pilots with, you know, 100 or so hours.

338
00:19:34,780 --> 00:19:38,440
But all of them say, you know, it's just like nothing else that they've ever flown.

339
00:19:38,700 --> 00:19:45,460
This airship combines characteristics from an airplane, a helicopter, and a balloon, all in one.

340
00:19:46,900 --> 00:19:54,040
What makes flying the Eureka unlike most aircraft is that its three 200-horsepower engines can rotate up to 120 degrees.

341
00:19:54,041 --> 00:19:58,740
Using joysticks, the pilot can control precisely how much each

342
00:19:58,840 --> 00:20:00,040
one pivots.

343
00:20:00,140 --> 00:20:01,840
We can go in a hover.

344
00:20:01,940 --> 00:20:05,780
That means we can stand still over a certain point

345
00:20:05,880 --> 00:20:09,720
and be able to make, like, left-right movements of 360,

346
00:20:09,820 --> 00:20:11,220
for example.

347
00:20:11,320 --> 00:20:13,320
That is similar to a helicopter.

348
00:20:13,420 --> 00:20:15,800
But the controls are way different.

349
00:20:15,900 --> 00:20:18,440
And so is the top speed.

350
00:20:18,540 --> 00:20:22,160
The Eureka maxes out at about 80 miles an hour.

351
00:20:22,161 --> 00:20:26,060
And though the pilot can take this ship to an altitude of 10,000

352
00:20:26,160 --> 00:20:30,300
feet, he's likely to stay much closer to the ground.

353
00:20:30,400 --> 00:20:33,900
That's because, like a child's balloon on a windy day,

354
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:37,840
an airship is at the mercy of air currents and thermals.

355
00:20:37,940 --> 00:20:40,280
And pilots have to adjust accordingly.

356
00:20:40,380 --> 00:20:43,340
You really have to feel what the air is doing to the ship.

357
00:20:43,440 --> 00:20:46,020
And it's not so much physically demanding to fly.

358
00:20:46,120 --> 00:20:48,520
Like the other blimps, they have pedals and wheels,

359
00:20:48,620 --> 00:20:50,920
and it's really physically demanding.

360
00:20:50,921 --> 00:20:52,420
This is far more mentally demanding.

361
00:20:52,520 --> 00:20:55,020
You really have to fly it by the seat of your pants.

362
00:20:55,120 --> 00:20:56,820
But for the few veteran Zeppelin pilots

363
00:20:56,920 --> 00:20:59,620
around the world, that's not the catch.

364
00:20:59,720 --> 00:21:01,560
That's the fun.

365
00:21:01,660 --> 00:21:05,260
If this driver's seat just seems too tame,

366
00:21:05,360 --> 00:21:07,600
buckle up tight for a stomach-turning flight

367
00:21:07,700 --> 00:21:10,180
in one of the deadliest flying machines on Earth.

368
00:21:12,680 --> 00:21:13,580
Make it impact.

369
00:21:13,680 --> 00:21:14,580
Target destroyed.

370
00:21:20,920 --> 00:21:27,920
Imagine a high-tech vehicle so difficult to operate

371
00:21:27,921 --> 00:21:32,920
that learning the ropes takes even the most experienced

372
00:21:32,921 --> 00:21:35,920
drivers two years.

373
00:21:35,921 --> 00:21:39,920
That's the challenge for every licensed helicopter pilot

374
00:21:39,921 --> 00:21:44,920
who wants to grab the reins of the US Army's AH-64 Apache

375
00:21:44,921 --> 00:21:45,920
longbow.

376
00:21:45,921 --> 00:21:47,420
in the back seat.

377
00:21:47,520 --> 00:21:49,460
The design was with the tandem seat

378
00:21:49,560 --> 00:21:51,620
and the back seat, or his primary focus is actually

379
00:21:51,720 --> 00:21:53,120
piloting or flying the aircraft, while the front seat

380
00:21:53,220 --> 00:21:55,560
is conducting the majority of the reconnaissance

381
00:21:55,660 --> 00:21:57,060
and attack operations.

382
00:21:57,160 --> 00:21:59,460
With the pilot seated behind the gunner,

383
00:21:59,560 --> 00:22:01,260
the Apache is ready for action.

384
00:22:01,360 --> 00:22:02,940
And on your ready or clear.

385
00:22:15,920 --> 00:22:18,160
Designed to protect ground troops by launching

386
00:22:18,260 --> 00:22:25,660
devastating assaults, this machine is a flying tank.

387
00:22:25,760 --> 00:22:27,360
The primary function of the Apache

388
00:22:27,460 --> 00:22:30,960
is to deal death and destruction on the battlefield.

389
00:22:31,060 --> 00:22:34,680
The Apache has a distinct honor being the most lethal attack

390
00:22:34,780 --> 00:22:37,480
helicopter in the United States inventory.

391
00:22:40,220 --> 00:22:43,720
We can carry more, go farther, go faster than any other

392
00:22:43,820 --> 00:22:44,520
helicopter.

393
00:22:45,920 --> 00:22:49,720
The Apache can soar as fast as 167 miles per hour

394
00:22:49,820 --> 00:22:53,660
and as high as 15,000 feet.

395
00:22:55,760 --> 00:22:58,660
It can also execute evasive maneuvers impossible

396
00:22:58,760 --> 00:23:02,160
for most helicopters, including barrel rolls and loops.

397
00:23:15,920 --> 00:23:21,300
Any pilot who begins training on the Apache has already earned his wings as a chopper

398
00:23:21,301 --> 00:23:22,300
pilot.

399
00:23:22,301 --> 00:23:32,740
But for those whose experience might be limited to the freeways, here's an idea of the basics.

400
00:23:32,741 --> 00:23:37,520
The key instrument in the cockpit is the cyclic control stick.

401
00:23:37,521 --> 00:23:42,200
It gives the pilot control over the helicopter's four rotor blades, which are essentially rotating

402
00:23:42,201 --> 00:23:44,920
wings.

403
00:23:44,921 --> 00:23:49,599
the stick causes the rotor blades to tilt, which changes how much lift they provide.

404
00:23:49,600 --> 00:23:50,300
Adjusting

405
00:23:50,301 --> 00:23:54,254
the tilt equally for all the blades lifts the helicopter straight up and down.

406
00:23:54,255 --> 00:23:54,955
Changing

407
00:23:54,956 --> 00:24:00,060
the tilt for some of the blades creates uneven lift, causing the helicopter to climb, dive,

408
00:24:00,061 --> 00:24:08,545
or turn. But controlling an Apache requires more than just skill.

409
00:24:08,546 --> 00:24:12,200
It also takes nerves. Flying

410
00:24:12,201 --> 00:24:17,520
this chopper is so sophisticated and the enemy territory traversed is so hostile that pilots

411
00:24:17,521 --> 00:24:25,400
refer to it as riding the dragon. In the eyes of the enemy, the Apache

412
00:24:25,401 --> 00:24:29,680
is a very dangerous helicopter. In the eyes of friendly forces, it's

413
00:24:29,681 --> 00:24:33,220
also a very, very dangerous aircraft to learn how to fly.

414
00:24:34,080 --> 00:24:38,460
Typically, an aviator will go through approximately anywhere from a year and a half to two years

415
00:24:38,461 --> 00:24:41,640
of flight training from start to finish to become an Apache pilot.

416
00:24:42,200 --> 00:24:46,200
One of the most difficult things about flying the Apache is having

417
00:24:46,300 --> 00:24:50,600
to constantly shift focus from one eye to the other,

418
00:24:50,700 --> 00:24:54,140
because the helmet is fitted with a monocle that's fixed in front

419
00:24:54,240 --> 00:24:55,840
of the pilot's right eye.

420
00:24:55,940 --> 00:24:59,860
12 different instrument readings from the cockpit

421
00:24:59,960 --> 00:25:02,260
are projected onto the monocle screen.

422
00:25:02,360 --> 00:25:04,660
Other images can be superimposed there,

423
00:25:04,760 --> 00:25:07,000
from those shot by the helicopter's cameras

424
00:25:07,100 --> 00:25:10,100
to radar information.

425
00:25:10,101 --> 00:25:14,100
While the pilot's right eye is interpreting all that data,

426
00:25:14,200 --> 00:25:16,900
his left has to be free to scan outside the aircraft

427
00:25:17,000 --> 00:25:18,500
for threats.

428
00:25:18,600 --> 00:25:21,540
All the while, sensors are detecting exactly where

429
00:25:21,640 --> 00:25:23,640
his monocled right eye is looking.

430
00:25:23,740 --> 00:25:26,680
Just move the monocle's crosshairs on a target below,

431
00:25:26,780 --> 00:25:29,680
and the weapons he selects will swivel and aim precisely

432
00:25:29,780 --> 00:25:30,660
where he's looking.

433
00:25:33,160 --> 00:25:36,060
I think the biggest thing that everybody asks us about

434
00:25:36,061 --> 00:25:39,560
asks us about is if the gun really does follow the line

435
00:25:39,660 --> 00:25:41,440
of sight of where the pilot's looking, and it absolutely

436
00:25:41,540 --> 00:25:42,240
does.

437
00:25:46,140 --> 00:25:48,600
The Apache's arsenal includes the M230

438
00:25:48,700 --> 00:25:52,440
automatic cannon mounted under the fuselage.

439
00:25:52,540 --> 00:25:55,980
It can take out a target more than two miles away.

440
00:25:56,080 --> 00:25:59,820
You can shoot in excess of 600 rounds a minute.

441
00:25:59,920 --> 00:26:02,820
We don't generally do that, but also, I like to compare it

442
00:26:02,821 --> 00:26:05,720
to compare it to each round has almost the capability

443
00:26:05,820 --> 00:26:08,520
of a single grenade.

444
00:26:08,620 --> 00:26:10,320
With the touch of a button, the pilot

445
00:26:10,420 --> 00:26:12,820
can also deploy a variety of smaller missiles

446
00:26:12,920 --> 00:26:15,960
and free-flight rockets, usually mounted and fired

447
00:26:16,060 --> 00:26:18,160
from underneath the wings.

448
00:26:18,260 --> 00:26:20,480
But the most powerful weapon at his disposal

449
00:26:20,580 --> 00:26:26,080
is the AGM-114, aptly known as the Hellfire Missile.

450
00:26:26,180 --> 00:26:29,120
This is a missile that will penetrate any known armor

451
00:26:29,220 --> 00:26:31,280
on the battlefield, and this thing will reach out

452
00:26:31,281 --> 00:26:33,620
and touch you five miles plus.

453
00:26:33,720 --> 00:26:36,620
Pinpoint accuracy.

454
00:26:36,720 --> 00:26:38,920
For all the onboard firepower,

455
00:26:39,020 --> 00:26:41,920
one of the most reassuring aspects of flying an Apache

456
00:26:42,020 --> 00:26:45,420
is the blanket of security that protects the crew.

457
00:26:45,520 --> 00:26:47,940
The windshield is made of reinforced glass

458
00:26:48,040 --> 00:26:51,640
that can withstand every type of small arms fire.

459
00:26:51,740 --> 00:26:55,140
And the pilot seat is covered in a heavy duty layer of Kevlar.

460
00:26:57,880 --> 00:26:59,680
This aircraft withstood significant damage

461
00:26:59,681 --> 00:27:02,880
in both Iraq and Afghanistan, a lot of small arms fire,

462
00:27:02,980 --> 00:27:04,920
RPG fire.

463
00:27:05,020 --> 00:27:06,920
I think some of the newer features that are just

464
00:27:07,020 --> 00:27:09,120
that make this aircraft as amazing as it is,

465
00:27:09,220 --> 00:27:12,160
is its capability to stay in the fight for long periods

466
00:27:12,260 --> 00:27:16,300
of time, its ability to take an excessive amount of enemy fire

467
00:27:16,400 --> 00:27:18,260
and still stay in the fight and provide that capability

468
00:27:18,360 --> 00:27:19,600
to guys that desperately need it.

469
00:27:24,380 --> 00:27:26,780
It's what makes the Apache one of the military's most

470
00:27:26,781 --> 00:27:36,080
advanced and most celebrated aircraft for a thrill ride

471
00:27:36,180 --> 00:27:38,820
without the heights and the two-year training course.

472
00:27:48,340 --> 00:27:51,280
Let's try on the wintry speed machine, owned by nearly two

473
00:27:51,380 --> 00:27:54,140
million Americans.

474
00:27:54,141 --> 00:27:57,780
The main allure to riding snowmobiles, jumping snowmobiles,

475
00:27:57,880 --> 00:28:01,740
hill climbing snowmobiles is the adrenaline rush.

476
00:28:01,840 --> 00:28:04,680
I think that's what drives most people.

477
00:28:04,780 --> 00:28:10,900
Everyone sets out goals and they want to jump higher,

478
00:28:11,000 --> 00:28:17,840
climb higher, go faster than they've ever even dreamed

479
00:28:17,940 --> 00:28:20,400
possible.

480
00:28:20,500 --> 00:28:23,700
Racing souped up models up to 150 miles per hour

481
00:28:23,701 --> 00:28:27,500
and riding big air like these experts

482
00:28:27,600 --> 00:28:31,400
isn't for the average weekend snowmobiler.

483
00:28:31,500 --> 00:28:37,940
But it's a ton of fun, even without pushing the envelope.

484
00:28:38,040 --> 00:28:40,260
And driving isn't that complicated.

485
00:28:40,360 --> 00:28:43,060
Unlike your car, snowmobiling is a little bit different

486
00:28:43,160 --> 00:28:44,460
where the controlling of the vehicle

487
00:28:44,560 --> 00:28:47,860
are done with your fingers as opposed to your feet.

488
00:28:47,960 --> 00:28:50,100
As you can see on my snowmobile, I've

489
00:28:50,200 --> 00:28:52,600
got a thumb lever for the gas.

490
00:28:52,601 --> 00:28:56,600
And then I also have a finger lever for your brake.

491
00:28:56,700 --> 00:28:59,140
Pressing down the gas lever engages

492
00:28:59,240 --> 00:29:03,940
the snowmobile's belt track, which

493
00:29:04,040 --> 00:29:06,240
operates on a friction system similar to the Bradley

494
00:29:06,340 --> 00:29:08,240
fighting vehicle.

495
00:29:08,340 --> 00:29:11,580
Once the belt track is engaged, how extreme the ride gets

496
00:29:11,680 --> 00:29:13,160
is up to the driver.

497
00:29:13,260 --> 00:29:15,660
There are virtually no limits in terms of climbing,

498
00:29:15,760 --> 00:29:17,260
no limits in terms of handling.

499
00:29:17,360 --> 00:29:21,600
And as far as jumping these things,

500
00:29:21,601 --> 00:29:24,940
I mean, they've got so much power.

501
00:29:25,040 --> 00:29:29,140
The only thing limiting a rider is their own nerve.

502
00:29:29,240 --> 00:29:31,640
You can carve these snowmobiles through deep powder,

503
00:29:31,740 --> 00:29:32,840
just like it was watered.

504
00:29:35,740 --> 00:29:37,240
And who needs snow?

505
00:29:37,340 --> 00:29:39,460
With a little creativity in the right parts,

506
00:29:39,560 --> 00:29:42,460
the snowmobile can be modified to drive virtually anywhere.

507
00:29:42,560 --> 00:29:45,060
There's truly nowhere a snowmobile can't go.

508
00:29:45,160 --> 00:29:49,160
You put a few wheels on the skis, a flat track,

509
00:29:49,260 --> 00:29:50,900
you can take it to the asphalt.

510
00:29:50,901 --> 00:29:55,540
You can extend the track, put a bigger lug on it,

511
00:29:55,640 --> 00:29:58,300
take it to the mountains, or you can take your seat off

512
00:29:58,400 --> 00:30:02,140
and use your standard track and walk across water.

513
00:30:02,240 --> 00:30:04,940
So with a few simple mods that anybody can do at home,

514
00:30:05,040 --> 00:30:07,960
a snowmobile can go anywhere.

515
00:30:08,060 --> 00:30:13,260
We'll compare the freedom of this driver's seat

516
00:30:13,360 --> 00:30:16,260
to a vehicle that's a real heavyweight.

517
00:30:16,360 --> 00:30:20,900
How does 8 million pounds of machine sound?

518
00:30:20,901 --> 00:30:27,900
Say hello to one of the largest mega machines on the planet,

519
00:30:27,901 --> 00:30:32,900
the 850 Dragline crane.

520
00:30:32,901 --> 00:30:38,900
The 850 Dragline, and two others just like it,

521
00:30:38,901 --> 00:30:42,900
do the heavy lifting at this phosphate mine in Aurora,

522
00:30:42,901 --> 00:30:47,900
North Carolina.

523
00:30:47,901 --> 00:30:51,040
in Aurora, North Carolina.

524
00:30:51,140 --> 00:30:55,640
This machine weighs a whopping 8 million pounds,

525
00:30:55,740 --> 00:31:01,740
thunders with 8,000 horsepower, and sports a boom as tall

526
00:31:01,840 --> 00:31:03,940
as the Statue of Liberty.

527
00:31:04,040 --> 00:31:05,660
The amazing thing about it is it's

528
00:31:05,760 --> 00:31:08,460
operated by one man in that little perch over there.

529
00:31:14,360 --> 00:31:16,060
Like the driver's seat of a car,

530
00:31:16,061 --> 00:31:18,700
The Dragline 850 is equipped with a windshield that allows

531
00:31:18,800 --> 00:31:21,800
the operator to watch out for any potential dangers.

532
00:31:21,900 --> 00:31:25,060
With one big difference, in a car,

533
00:31:25,160 --> 00:31:28,400
his attention is usually directed ahead.

534
00:31:28,500 --> 00:31:30,600
Here, he needs to broaden his focus.

535
00:31:30,700 --> 00:31:32,100
In a car, you're kind of looking forward

536
00:31:32,200 --> 00:31:33,720
to see what the next driver's going to do.

537
00:31:33,820 --> 00:31:35,420
And here, you don't have to worry about that.

538
00:31:35,520 --> 00:31:36,620
You're kind of always looking down

539
00:31:36,720 --> 00:31:38,120
and looking off to your right or left

540
00:31:38,220 --> 00:31:41,720
out of these two windows to see if anyone has pulled within,

541
00:31:41,820 --> 00:31:43,420
up in your swing path.

542
00:31:43,421 --> 00:31:46,720
Right here, this is kind of like your car steering wheel

543
00:31:46,820 --> 00:31:49,120
right here and your brake pedal down here.

544
00:31:49,220 --> 00:31:51,060
The foot pedals swing the enormous boom

545
00:31:51,160 --> 00:31:52,060
from side to side.

546
00:31:57,960 --> 00:32:01,280
Levers on the left control the boom's vertical movement.

547
00:32:01,380 --> 00:32:04,880
And a panel on the right manipulates the giant bucket.

548
00:32:04,980 --> 00:32:08,720
Every scoop is 52 cubic yards of dirt and rocks,

549
00:32:08,820 --> 00:32:11,020
enough to fill a two-car garage.

550
00:32:11,021 --> 00:32:15,960
In order to be competitive in this business, you have to move a lot of materials.

551
00:32:16,180 --> 00:32:20,520
And so the key to it is the amount of ore that these big guys can pull out of the ground.

552
00:32:22,200 --> 00:32:29,540
Every scoop contains phosphate, a substance vital to the production of everything from toothpaste to soft drinks to flat screen TVs.

553
00:32:30,840 --> 00:32:37,720
Mining it all is a costly endeavor, and a huge chunk of that expense is the electricity that powers each of the mine's three drag lines.

554
00:32:37,721 --> 00:32:39,960
It's all electric.

555
00:32:40,780 --> 00:32:42,840
So our electric bill here at Aurora

556
00:32:42,841 --> 00:32:44,920
is around $3 million a month,

557
00:32:45,180 --> 00:32:47,960
and this machine consumes a large part of that.

558
00:32:48,900 --> 00:32:50,520
Who knew consuming electricity

559
00:32:50,521 --> 00:32:51,860
could be so much fun?

560
00:32:53,700 --> 00:32:55,680
Oh, yeah, it's fun to get on them.

561
00:32:55,960 --> 00:32:57,860
I mean, it's like being on a battleship

562
00:32:57,861 --> 00:32:59,780
when you're in the back and you can't see out

563
00:32:59,781 --> 00:33:01,020
and it's rotating around.

564
00:33:01,260 --> 00:33:03,840
So, yeah, they're definitely amazing pieces of equipment.

565
00:33:03,841 --> 00:33:08,840
This driver's seat may be a bucket full of fun.

566
00:33:15,380 --> 00:33:17,880
But out on the farm, this machine

567
00:33:17,980 --> 00:33:20,900
is the pick of the crop.

568
00:33:21,000 --> 00:33:25,300
It's the 32-ton custom-built 440-horsepower carrot

569
00:33:25,400 --> 00:33:28,500
harvester designed and used by Bolthouse Farms

570
00:33:28,600 --> 00:33:29,800
in Bakersfield, California.

571
00:33:33,840 --> 00:33:40,840
This mega machine is the only one of its kind,

572
00:33:40,940 --> 00:33:44,880
and driving it might seem highly complicated.

573
00:33:44,980 --> 00:33:48,380
But the truth is, the Bolthouse Harvester

574
00:33:48,480 --> 00:33:50,080
virtually drives itself.

575
00:33:52,300 --> 00:33:54,400
We just turn on the machine pretty good,

576
00:33:54,500 --> 00:33:57,400
and we turn on your GPS and your screen,

577
00:33:57,500 --> 00:33:59,800
make sure everything's OK.

578
00:33:59,801 --> 00:34:02,500
You turn on your GPS and your screen,

579
00:34:02,600 --> 00:34:07,100
make sure everything's OK, and we're ready to go.

580
00:34:07,200 --> 00:34:09,140
Like most high-tech harvesters today,

581
00:34:09,240 --> 00:34:12,340
this giant carrot plucker has a specialized GPS navigation

582
00:34:12,440 --> 00:34:14,940
system that keeps it almost flawlessly aligned

583
00:34:15,040 --> 00:34:17,860
with every road. So the driver can just sit back

584
00:34:17,960 --> 00:34:20,460
and enjoy the three-mile-per-hour ride.

585
00:34:20,560 --> 00:34:23,960
You're steering, you're steering free.

586
00:34:24,060 --> 00:34:26,360
You don't got to use steering no more.

587
00:34:26,460 --> 00:34:28,260
If he does have to make an adjustment,

588
00:34:28,261 --> 00:34:31,560
it's always tiny. And all it takes is one finger

589
00:34:31,660 --> 00:34:35,200
on this touch screen. Unlike driving a car,

590
00:34:35,300 --> 00:34:37,500
the trick is to not keep eyes on the road,

591
00:34:37,600 --> 00:34:39,500
but on the instruments.

592
00:34:39,600 --> 00:34:42,800
Well, in a car, you got to see, because you're looking straight.

593
00:34:42,900 --> 00:34:44,600
On these machines, you got to look down,

594
00:34:44,700 --> 00:34:47,180
because you got to keep it pretty much straight

595
00:34:47,280 --> 00:34:49,720
to see where you're at. So see, like right now, right here,

596
00:34:49,820 --> 00:34:52,420
I'm moving just about a half an inch to my left.

597
00:34:52,520 --> 00:34:55,520
So I got to go half an inch to the right,

598
00:34:55,620 --> 00:34:57,720
so I could get lined up, so I could pick up

599
00:34:57,721 --> 00:34:59,220
from the front end pretty good.

600
00:35:02,560 --> 00:35:04,420
Another big difference between a car

601
00:35:04,520 --> 00:35:08,460
and the bolthouse harvester is when it reaches the end

602
00:35:08,560 --> 00:35:12,160
of the field and the driver needs to stop.

603
00:35:12,260 --> 00:35:13,960
Don't look for the brake pedal.

604
00:35:14,060 --> 00:35:15,280
There isn't one.

605
00:35:15,380 --> 00:35:16,800
That's because the harvester is equipped

606
00:35:16,900 --> 00:35:19,480
with a hydrostatic transmission.

607
00:35:19,580 --> 00:35:21,640
That means pressure from hydraulic fluid is used

608
00:35:21,740 --> 00:35:24,280
to turn the drive motors.

609
00:35:24,380 --> 00:35:27,720
To brake, the driver just eases off the throttle.

610
00:35:27,721 --> 00:35:29,420
And the flow of fluid to the drive motors

611
00:35:29,520 --> 00:35:32,020
is reduced or stopped.

612
00:35:32,120 --> 00:35:33,520
So it's similar to a forklift.

613
00:35:33,620 --> 00:35:36,320
What we're able to do is by throttling it up and back

614
00:35:36,420 --> 00:35:38,260
and slowly putting it into reverse,

615
00:35:38,360 --> 00:35:41,160
we actually use it as negative pressure, which creates us

616
00:35:41,260 --> 00:35:42,360
to be able to stop it.

617
00:35:49,780 --> 00:35:51,180
When it's rolling, the driver gets

618
00:35:51,280 --> 00:35:52,820
a little help from an operator riding

619
00:35:52,920 --> 00:35:55,220
on the rear of the harvester.

620
00:35:55,320 --> 00:35:57,220
It's his job to make sure the conveyor system

621
00:35:57,221 --> 00:36:01,220
the carrots is operating as designed.

622
00:36:01,320 --> 00:36:03,020
By the time the workday is done,

623
00:36:03,120 --> 00:36:06,220
they've picked 500,000 pounds of carrots,

624
00:36:06,320 --> 00:36:09,260
enough to fill 2.7 million three-ounce bags

625
00:36:09,360 --> 00:36:11,860
for kids' lunchboxes.

626
00:36:11,960 --> 00:36:16,680
If driving 32 tons of vehicles seems like a handful,

627
00:36:16,780 --> 00:36:19,880
this next seat weighs just a fraction of that

628
00:36:19,980 --> 00:36:21,880
and travels at breakneck speeds.

629
00:36:21,881 --> 00:36:23,880
with the big neck speeds.

630
00:36:32,520 --> 00:36:36,920
Some of the fastest and most dangerous rides around

631
00:36:37,020 --> 00:36:38,640
are experienced from the driver's seats

632
00:36:38,740 --> 00:36:43,040
of two utterly different vehicles, speed boats

633
00:36:43,140 --> 00:36:45,140
and airplanes.

634
00:36:45,240 --> 00:36:47,040
Combining the best elements of each

635
00:36:47,140 --> 00:36:50,040
results in one of the wildest rides of all.

636
00:36:50,041 --> 00:36:51,880
driver's seat of a hydroplane.

637
00:36:58,920 --> 00:37:00,320
These boats are called hydroplanes,

638
00:37:00,420 --> 00:37:03,360
so hydro for the water and plane for the air.

639
00:37:03,460 --> 00:37:05,920
Here at Mission Bay in San Diego, California,

640
00:37:10,000 --> 00:37:13,200
professional hydroplane drivers are dueling for top honors

641
00:37:13,300 --> 00:37:15,540
at the World Series of Powerboat Racing.

642
00:37:15,541 --> 00:37:21,240
racing. But first, the driver has to squeeze into a cockpit so

643
00:37:21,241 --> 00:37:25,380
small, the steering wheel has to come out before he can go in.

644
00:37:25,381 --> 00:37:28,980
So as you can see in the cockpit, it seats one,

645
00:37:28,981 --> 00:37:31,620
barely. The cramped

646
00:37:31,621 --> 00:37:34,300
conditions are similar to the ones found behind the wheel of a

647
00:37:34,301 --> 00:37:39,500
Formula One or Indy-style race car. And just like race car

648
00:37:39,501 --> 00:37:43,900
drivers, behind the wheel of a hydroplane, standard attire is a

649
00:37:43,901 --> 00:37:44,900
suit.

650
00:37:44,901 --> 00:37:49,740
We have to have a fireproof suit just in case it catches on fire.

651
00:37:49,741 --> 00:37:52,440
We also have to have the shoes and the socks and the whole deal.

652
00:37:52,441 --> 00:37:54,400
Next is a helmet, certified helmet.

653
00:37:54,401 --> 00:37:55,900
You've got to have a crash lid.

654
00:37:55,901 --> 00:38:02,260
And in my case, I came across these visors from the military that I've mounted onto my

655
00:38:02,261 --> 00:38:03,900
helmet in case there's glare.

656
00:38:03,901 --> 00:38:06,220
You know, I've got a good visor.

657
00:38:06,221 --> 00:38:10,900
Underneath the helmet, he's wearing form-fitted earpieces that protect his ears from engine

658
00:38:10,901 --> 00:38:14,780
noise and keep him in constant contact with his team.

659
00:38:14,781 --> 00:38:18,880
It's also works real good to keep the water out of your ears

660
00:38:18,881 --> 00:38:20,100
if you're upside down.

661
00:38:20,101 --> 00:38:22,480
Unfortunately, sometimes that'll happen.

662
00:38:22,481 --> 00:38:24,440
Anyone in the driver's seat of a hydroplane

663
00:38:24,441 --> 00:38:29,320
also wears a specialized neck brace called a Hans device,

664
00:38:29,321 --> 00:38:31,400
which keeps the head and neck secure

665
00:38:31,401 --> 00:38:33,720
during even the most brutal crashes.

666
00:38:33,721 --> 00:38:35,140
Sometimes we're in so tight, it's

667
00:38:35,141 --> 00:38:39,660
hard to breathe at first, but you get used to it.

668
00:38:39,661 --> 00:38:42,560
And then last but not least, very critical, is our air mask.

669
00:38:42,660 --> 00:38:45,860
And it's like an F-16 fighter mask.

670
00:38:45,960 --> 00:38:47,500
Got my communication in here.

671
00:38:47,600 --> 00:38:49,400
It seals really tight to my face.

672
00:38:49,500 --> 00:38:51,600
Because we are upside down, the cockpits

673
00:38:51,700 --> 00:38:52,800
will fill up with water.

674
00:39:00,520 --> 00:39:01,920
The only thing hydroplane drivers

675
00:39:02,020 --> 00:39:04,920
need more than safety is stamina.

676
00:39:05,020 --> 00:39:06,220
It's just physically exhausting

677
00:39:06,320 --> 00:39:08,960
to be able to turn the steering wheel, run the canard wings,

678
00:39:08,961 --> 00:39:11,860
and just physically hold on and keep your head up

679
00:39:11,960 --> 00:39:15,200
and keep your focus is unbelievably exhausting.

680
00:39:19,900 --> 00:39:21,600
You know, it's not a paved track like you'd find

681
00:39:21,700 --> 00:39:23,700
typical in auto racing.

682
00:39:23,800 --> 00:39:26,780
It's very bumpy, and you're going through a turn at anywhere

683
00:39:26,880 --> 00:39:29,840
between three to four and a half Gs.

684
00:39:29,940 --> 00:39:32,280
It becomes extremely violent.

685
00:39:32,380 --> 00:39:35,760
Traveling at a max speed of 200 plus miles per hour,

686
00:39:35,860 --> 00:39:38,960
a hydroplane is the fastest thing on water.

687
00:39:38,961 --> 00:39:42,360
Though as the driver of one of these crafts knows, very little

688
00:39:42,460 --> 00:39:44,360
of the vehicle actually touches the water.

689
00:39:46,500 --> 00:39:48,200
In fact, the hydroplane is designed

690
00:39:48,300 --> 00:39:50,700
to keep as little of the boat in contact with the surface

691
00:39:50,800 --> 00:39:52,700
as possible.

692
00:39:52,800 --> 00:39:56,320
Essentially, it flies over the water.

693
00:39:56,420 --> 00:39:58,220
Here's how it works.

694
00:39:58,320 --> 00:40:02,620
The bottom of the craft is flat, but the deck is a smooth curve

695
00:40:02,720 --> 00:40:05,320
shaped like the edge of an airplane wing.

696
00:40:05,420 --> 00:40:08,720
The design forces oncoming air to travel slowly

697
00:40:08,721 --> 00:40:12,960
slower over the top of the craft than the bottom, creating lift.

698
00:40:12,961 --> 00:40:15,960
Ideally, only the propeller remains in the water

699
00:40:15,961 --> 00:40:18,260
to provide propulsion.

700
00:40:18,261 --> 00:40:21,960
A hydroplane relies on air pressure to run.

701
00:40:21,961 --> 00:40:24,060
So it's got a tunnel in the middle,

702
00:40:24,061 --> 00:40:25,880
and the sponsor's on the outside.

703
00:40:25,881 --> 00:40:27,600
And the air rushing through there actually

704
00:40:27,601 --> 00:40:31,480
suspends the boat above the water.

705
00:40:31,481 --> 00:40:34,420
And due to the fact that we got a lot of aerodynamics,

706
00:40:34,421 --> 00:40:37,020
we have a front wing now that we run with our left foot.

707
00:40:37,021 --> 00:40:39,360
And I can actually bring this thing down and create lift

708
00:40:39,460 --> 00:40:41,260
and get the nose of the boat to come up, which is pretty

709
00:40:41,360 --> 00:40:42,760
dangerous, especially when it's windy.

710
00:40:42,860 --> 00:40:43,960
And then if we get going too fast,

711
00:40:44,060 --> 00:40:45,060
I can just bring it back down.

712
00:40:45,160 --> 00:40:48,460
So we literally just fly the boat.

713
00:40:48,560 --> 00:40:51,740
But the craft's aerodynamic shape

714
00:40:51,840 --> 00:40:55,100
and its front wing aren't the only design features

715
00:40:55,200 --> 00:41:00,080
that make the experience behind the wheel less about boating

716
00:41:00,180 --> 00:41:00,980
and more about flying.

717
00:41:00,981 --> 00:41:05,880
flying.

718
00:41:05,980 --> 00:41:08,320
The most commonly used engine in hydroplanes

719
00:41:08,420 --> 00:41:12,600
is a 3,000 horsepower turbine, the same one used on the Army's

720
00:41:12,700 --> 00:41:17,060
hulking Chinook helicopters.

721
00:41:17,160 --> 00:41:20,200
As this driver races atop the water at more than 200 miles

722
00:41:20,300 --> 00:41:23,360
per hour, he's riding a fine edge between control...

723
00:41:23,460 --> 00:41:26,440
Jeff Bernard crashes out at the end of that one.

724
00:41:26,540 --> 00:41:30,980
...and catastrophe.

725
00:41:30,981 --> 00:41:34,820
generate too much lift, and the results could be fatal.

726
00:41:38,820 --> 00:41:42,500
In July 2010, at a race on the Detroit River,

727
00:41:42,600 --> 00:41:45,820
a malfunction sent JW careening toward a seawall

728
00:41:45,920 --> 00:41:50,640
crowded with spectators at 70 miles per hour.

729
00:41:50,740 --> 00:41:52,640
The wreck could have been life-threatening,

730
00:41:52,740 --> 00:41:54,640
but thanks to the arsenal of safety equipment

731
00:41:54,740 --> 00:41:59,040
inside the driver's seat, JW only suffered a broken foot.

732
00:41:59,041 --> 00:42:00,540
I'm extremely lucky.

733
00:42:00,640 --> 00:42:04,340
The cockpits that we have now are unbelievably safe.

734
00:42:04,440 --> 00:42:06,740
You know, nobody, when they engineered these cockpits,

735
00:42:06,840 --> 00:42:09,780
never, ever would have thought of a scenario going straight

736
00:42:09,880 --> 00:42:11,780
into a wall.

737
00:42:11,880 --> 00:42:13,880
Yeah, I got a broken foot, but that's a big deal.

738
00:42:18,500 --> 00:42:24,400
On the glassy water, over rough terrain,

739
00:42:24,500 --> 00:42:28,000
and through smoky skies, some of the most action-packed

740
00:42:28,001 --> 00:42:34,240
most action-packed moments in life happen in the driver's seat.

741
00:42:34,340 --> 00:42:38,240
All it takes is an expertly designed vehicle, a mastery

742
00:42:38,340 --> 00:42:42,040
of the controls, and the will to push the limits.

743
00:42:42,140 --> 00:42:47,420
And a driver can do amazing things without ever

744
00:42:47,520 --> 00:42:49,420
leaving the seat.

